      "writtenOn": "2022-09-14T09:20:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Implemented : you can see it in patchset 10. I think it\u0027s not as good.\n\nThe main problem is the complexity arising from interactions between the new meaning of the resource and the NETWORK_AVOID_BAD_WIFI setting. See my documentation for it :\n\n    [description of behavior for -1 and 1]\n    If this is 0, starting with Android U, the behavior is the same as -1. On Android\n    T and below, the behavior is similar to -1, with the exception that the device\n    will not aggressively use a broken Wi-Fi network when connecting to it. It will,\n    however, stay connected to a Wi-Fi network that loses Internet access, even if\n    cellular connectivity is available.\n\n    Note that the Settings.Global.NETWORK_AVOID_BAD_WIFI can be used to override the\n    value of this setting to some extent.\n    If that setting has value NETWORK_AVOID_BAD_WIFI_IGNORE \u003d 0, then the device will\n    prefer bad Wi-Fis to good cellular ; whether it aggressively switches to it\n    depends on the value of this setting (1 behaves like 0).\n    [more on the two other options]\n\nSo now the config matters even if the setting is present, and setting\u003dIGNORE and config\u003dAVOID has to mean something, and that in turn must depend on the SDK level, so 🤯\n\nI chose to revert this to the original design of having a separate config.",
      "parentUuid": "4bb0d99c_2c84785f",
